.TH WAIT 2P "August 1, 1992"
.ds ]W POSIX 1003.1
.SH NAME
wait, waitpid, WIFEXITED, WEXITSTATUS, WIFSIGNALED, WTERMSIG, WIFSTOPPED, WSTOPSIG \- (POSIX version) wait for process to terminate or stop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.ft B
#include <sys/types.h>
.br
.B #include <sys/wait.h>
.PP
.B int wait(int *\fIstat_loc\fP);
.PP
.B int waitpid(pid_t \fIpid\fP, int *\fIstat_loc\fP,
.B int \fIoptions\fP);
.LP
.B WIFEXITED(int \fIstat_val\fR)
.LP
.B WEXITSTATUS(int \fIstat_val\fR)
.LP
.B WIFSIGNALED(int \fIstat_val\fR)
.LP
.B WTERMSIG(int \fIstat_val\fR)
.LP
.B WIFSTOPPED(int \fIstat_val\fR)
.LP
.B WSTOPSIG(int \fIstat_val\fR)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The information in this specification applies
only to POSIX applications.
.PP
This is the POSIX specification for \fIstat\fR.
For the BSD specification, see \fIstat\fR(2).
.PP
The \fIwait\fP function delays its caller until a signal is received or one
of its child processes terminates or stops due to tracing.  If any child has
died or stopped due to tracing and this has not been reported using
\fIwait\fP, return is immediate, returning the process ID
and exit status of one of those children.  If that child had died, it is
discarded.  If there are no children, return is immediate with the value \-1
returned.  If there are only running or stopped but reported children, the
calling process is blocked.
.LP
If \fIstat_loc\fP is not a NULL pointer, then on return from a successful
\fIwait\fP call the status of the child process whose process ID
is the return value of \fIwait\fP is stored in the int pointed to by
\fIstat_loc\fP.  The \fIstat_loc\fP argument to \fIwait)\fP and \fIwaitpid()\fP
(\fIstat_val\fP is the value pointed to by \fIstat_loc\fP) indicates the cause
of termination and other information about the terminated process in the
following manner:
.LP
.RS
.TP 5
\-
If the low-order 8 bits of \fIstat_val\fP are equal to 0177, the child process
has stopped; see \fIptrace(2)\fP and \fIsigvec(2)\fP.
.TP 5
\-
If the low-order 8 bits of \fIstat_val\fP are non-zero and are not equal to
0177, the child process terminated due to a signal; the low-order 7 bits of
\fIstat_val\fP contain the number of the signal that terminated the process.
In addition, if the low-order seventh bit of \fIstat_val\fP (that is, bit 0200)
is set, a ``core image'' of the process was produced; see \fIsigvec(2)\fP.
.TP 5
\-
Otherwise, the child process terminated due to an \fIexit(2)\fP call; the 8 bits
higher up from the low-order 8 bits of \fIstat_val\fP contain the low-order 8
bits of the argument that the child process passed to \fIexit(2)\fP.
.RE
.LP
The \fIoptions\fP argument is constructed from the bitwise inclusive OR of zero
or more of the following flags, defined in the header \fI<sys/wait.h>\fP.
.TP 25
WNOHANG
The \fIwaitpid()\fP function will not suspend execution of the calling process
if status is not immediately available for one of the child processes specified
by \fIpid\fP.
.TP 25
WUNTRACED
The status of any child processes specified by \fIpid\fP that are stopped and 
whose status has not yet been reported since they stopped, will also be
reported to the requesting process.
.LP
.IR WIFEXITED \s0,
.IR WEXITSTATUS \s0,
.IR WIFSIGNALED \s0,
.IR WTERMSIG \s0,
.IR WIFSTOPPED \s0,
\fRand\fP
.IR WSTOPSIG \s0,
are macros that take an argument \fIstat_val\fP as
returned by \fIwait\fP or \fIwaitpid()\fP.
.TP 25
WIFEXITED(stat_val)
evaluates to a nonzero value if status was returned for a child process that
terminated normally.
.LP
.TP 25
WEXITSTATUS(stat_val)
If the value of \fIWIFEXITED(stat_val\fP) is nonzero, this macro evaluates to
the low-order 8 bits of the \fIstatus\fP argument that the child process passed
to \fI_exit(2)\fP or \fIexit(2)\fP, or the value the child process returned
from \fImain()\fP.
.TP 25
WIFSIGNALED(stat_val)
evaluates to true when the process was terminated with a signal.
.TP 25
WTERMSIG(stat_val)
If the value of \fIWIFSIGNALED(stat_val\fP) is nonzero, this macro evaluates to
the number of the signal that caused the termination of the child process.
.TP 25
WIFSTOPPED(stat_val)
evaluates to true (1) when the process for which the \fIwait\fP or
\fIwaitpid()\fP call was made is stopped, or to false (0) otherwise.
.TP 25
WSTOPSIG(stat_val)
If the value of \fIWIFSTOPPED(stat_val\fP) is nonzero, this macro evaluates to
the number of the signal that caused the child process to stop.
.SH NOTES
If a parent process terminates without waiting on its children,
the initialization process (process ID = 1) inherits the children.
.SH "RETURN VALUE
If \fIwait\fP or \fIwaitpid()\fP returns due to a stopped or terminated child
process, the process ID of the child is returned to the calling process.
Otherwise, a value of \-1 is returned and \fIerrno\fP is set to indicate the
error.
.LP
\fIwaitpid()\fP returns 0 if
WNOHANG
is specified and there are no stopped or exited children, and return the
process ID of the child process if it returns due to a stopped or terminated
child process.  Otherwise, it returns a value of \-1 and set \fIerrno\fP to
indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
If any of the following conditions occur, the \fIwait\fP and \fIwaitpid()\fP
functions will return \-1 and set \fIerrno\fR to the corresponding value:
.TP 15
[ECHILD]
The calling process has no existing unwaited-for
child processes.
.TP 15
[EFAULT]
The
\fIstat_loc\fP 
argument points to an illegal address.
.TP 15
[EINTR]
The function was interrupted by a signal.  The value of the location pointed
to by \fIstat_loc\fP is undefined.
.PP
Additionally, \fIwaitpid()\fR will fail if:
.TP 15
[EINVAL]
The value of the \fIoptions\fP argument is not valid.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
exit(2), getrusage(2), sigvec(2), siginterrupt(3), signal(3), wait(2)
